            <ArticleTitle>Neurologic complications of phenylpropanolamine use.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>650-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eleven patients had neurologic symptoms after taking &quot;look-alike&quot; pills thought to contain amphetamines but actually containing phenylpropanolamine. Phenylpropanolamine, a nonregulated drug, is structurally and functionally similar to amphetamines. Acute onset of headache, psychiatric symptoms, or seizures in young patients may be due to phenylpropanolamine use.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
